#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1817476-c874-54ce-bb1c-b139692b2055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171434} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9ef030-2ae8-5173-9726-bba3c19a5d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171435} {:answer/id #uuid "1690235b-7a2e-5372-8797-2b40af0ccaf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171436} {:answer/id #uuid "628615cd-f7df-5bab-9721-9107b2108df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171437} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8dc918-029e-5269-a50b-636aeaa894d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1690235b-7a2e-5372-8797-2b40af0ccaf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171436} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd56b008-b99a-519a-9a0f-309647444b43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37d38f20-8a2f-5a94-a0f5-f641f4040cd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97542" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144{cos}^{2}⁡2t+144{sin}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=3\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={2}^{2}\\cdot 3=12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15f4d60-adc8-5f48-93d0-28bd34b73911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383658} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7602ad-c2b9-51a6-b631-7002a8be6489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383659} {:answer/id #uuid "98f7da3d-695b-564d-a747-2c679c13e042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383660} {:answer/id #uuid "67d8e66b-06f1-5a2a-bbe3-fe6b8b1c4af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383661} {:answer/id #uuid "b37a6afd-5db8-50e9-961e-b4f414b5f20c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b37a6afd-5db8-50e9-961e-b4f414b5f20c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383662} :answer-field/id #uuid "a74df77b-4150-572f-9be4-27e6f134b802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f995a141-d20e-5cb4-a7c5-cd9ca5ffca15" :question/math-academy-id "q-97640" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100{sin}^{2}⁡2t+100{cos}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100({sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $10\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=5\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=5\\cdot |2|=10\\text{ m/s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5fee7da-6127-585a-97ea-f2c5a3223789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383665} {:answer/id #uuid "f3fa6663-8feb-5ad2-ba1a-4930a76f7e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383666} {:answer/id #uuid "b5585aff-8803-5b22-8c1e-10672966d7b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i+5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383667} {:answer/id #uuid "7242a4c6-56f5-50ba-98a5-8cb5a5f40104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383668} {:answer/id #uuid "2e714924-30fe-5199-b18e-07fe3083024b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,i-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3fa6663-8feb-5ad2-ba1a-4930a76f7e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383666} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4fab373-9ee1-5e7e-a84b-8966c502f0a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af2083e0-3aeb-5fcd-b845-0a22c5b82e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97664" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $1\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(5,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=5$ and $\\omega =1\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,j \\\\  & =-5\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15fcce13-48c6-5960-badd-3dd42e6d880a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}i+4\\sqrt{2}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383672} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb3d382-8090-59c2-9dad-ab4eb83382ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383673} {:answer/id #uuid "35411343-0b0a-526d-8c1a-315e6230dd93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\sqrt{2}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383674} {:answer/id #uuid "8986bcae-209d-5922-9f41-86f75dcc8433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}i-4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383675} {:answer/id #uuid "80023cdb-5d7d-5efc-9e16-1785239ad288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4i+4\\sqrt{2}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15fcce13-48c6-5960-badd-3dd42e6d880a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}i+4\\sqrt{2}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383672} :answer-field/id #uuid "3455a640-7273-5520-909a-98c567b13358" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba9f4197-bf38-5147-a088-adef74f86d38" :question/math-academy-id "q-97666" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(4,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=4$ and $\\omega =2\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\\,j \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}\\,i+4\\sqrt{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4808dc6-0ae7-5ba9-ab08-c458e6ae63db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383679} {:answer/id #uuid "8fdbe17d-914d-52b9-88d8-79ab2652cb56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383680} {:answer/id #uuid "3f187c12-f580-54c1-8168-8a3d342e82be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383681} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0adcb8-b3cc-5956-8f77-c047b1f19b9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383682} {:answer/id #uuid "d6258473-e368-54d1-a430-ff47e31031fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6258473-e368-54d1-a430-ff47e31031fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383683} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f709cf3-8702-56fb-b86d-447224d66cb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c4456dd-d406-5a8c-9a07-35ca3a15f591" :question/math-academy-id "q-97674" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $0.5\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(4,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\pi $ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=4$ and $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & ={(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j)}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j \\\\ a(t) & ={(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\pi ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
